kunst_dk_denmark_6dba7d4003de,kunst_dk_denmark,https://www.kunst.dk/english/funding/different-art-forms,https://www.kunst.dk/english/funding-programmes,Danish Arts Foundation,"It is possible to apply for funding for artistic work that breaks with traditional genres or that is simply hard to define. In the Danish Arts Foundation, we aim to support creative thinking and innovative ideas. However, this sort of artistic work does not necessarily fit into the categories we have used to classify art forms and our committees. Nevertheless, artistic work that embraces different art forms is as important as more traditional art. Therefore, the Danish Art Foundation’s committees welcome all applications that border on or go beyond traditional art forms.",different art forms,funding,Denmark,Copenhagen,,,,"It is possible to apply for funding for artistic work that breaks with traditional genres or that is simply hard to define. In the Danish Arts Foundation, we aim to support creative thinking and innovative ideas. However, this sort of artistic work does not necessarily fit into the categories we have used to classify art forms and our committees. Nevertheless, artistic work that embraces different art forms is as important as more traditional art. Therefore, the Danish Art Foundation’s committees welcome all applications that border on or go beyond traditional art forms.",different art forms,funding,,Denmark,Copenhagen,['Multidisciplinary'],['Grant/Funding'],[],['Denmark'],[],,,,,,en,Different art forms,"Your application must obviously meet the general requirements for the funding programme that your application is directed to. However, beside these requirements, there are some additional guidelines for how to apply, when your work combines different art forms.

When you apply for project funding
If your application primarily concerns one specific art form and only to a lesser extent includes others, you should only submit one application. In this case, submit your application to the funding program you find most relevant.

If your project combines two or more different art forms on equal terms, you may split up your project into separate subprojects and submit separate applications for each subproject to different funding programmes.

When you break down your project into several applications, it is important that you also split up your budget, since you are not able to get the same expenses covered by more than one funding programme. Additionally, you must remember to state clearly that you have applied for funding from other programmes within the Danish Arts Foundation, with applications relating to other parts of your projects.

If you find it difficult to split up your project, you have to decide on a programme that you submit one application to, which covers the entire project.",Different art forms,"Your application must obviously meet the general requirements for the funding programme that your application is directed to. However, beside these requirements, there are some additional guidelines for how to apply, when your work combines different art forms.

When you apply for project funding
If your application primarily concerns one specific art form and only to a lesser extent includes others, you should only submit one application. In this case, submit your application to the funding program you find most relevant.

If your project combines two or more different art forms on equal terms, you may split up your project into separate subprojects and submit separate applications for each subproject to different funding programmes.

When you break down your project into several applications, it is important that you also split up your budget, since you are not able to get the same expenses covered by more than one funding programme. Additionally, you must remember to state clearly that you have applied for funding from other programmes within the Danish Arts Foundation, with applications relating to other parts of your projects.

If you find it difficult to split up your project, you have to decide on a programme that you submit one application to, which covers the entire project.",,,2026-09-24 09:58:10.893937+00:00
kunsthalle_exnergasse_vienna_59b7712c4d41,kunsthalle_exnergasse_vienna,https://www.wuk.at/fileadmin/user_upload/Media_Library/Bilder/KEX/Allgemeine_Bilder/kex_Plaene_und_Technik/kex-application_modalities_en.pdf,,kex—kunsthalle exnergasse,"We are looking for innovative, socially relevant and experimental exhibition projects in a variety of formats.",exhibition projects,open call,Austria,Vienna,,,,"We are looking for innovative, socially relevant and experimental exhibition projects in a variety of formats.",exhibition projects,open call,,Austria,Vienna,['Multidisciplinary'],['Open Call'],[],['Austria'],[],,,,,,en,kex—open call for Exhibition Projects for 2027,"Inter(national) group exhibitions can be submitted.
However, solo exhibitions and projects that only involve students will not be considered.

The online submitted proposals are limited to a text length of 4,000 characters (including blanks) maximum and should emphasize and explain the idea behind your exhibition concept. Submissions for solo exhibitions and exhibitions with students only will not be reviewed.
We also ask for short CVs of the person/s submitting the project as well as of the proposed artists and participants (maximum of 2,000 characters including blanks in total). In the first application phase, the concept is more important than individual biographies.
The submission should therefore not exceed a total of 6,000 characters including spaces (approx. 900 words).
Furthermore we kindly ask you to visualize your project proposal or idea by selecting/designing one exemplary image (photo, sketch, collage, etc.) of approx. 20x30 cm size (DIN A4) that has to be uploaded as PDF-file with a maximum size of 50 MB.
Text overlengths are not taken into account! Multi-page PDF's are not considered!",kex—open call for Exhibition Projects for 2027,"Inter(national) group exhibitions can be submitted.
However, solo exhibitions and projects that only involve students will not be considered.

The online submitted proposals are limited to a text length of 4,000 characters (including blanks) maximum and should emphasize and explain the idea behind your exhibition concept. Submissions for solo exhibitions and exhibitions with students only will not be reviewed.
We also ask for short CVs of the person/s submitting the project as well as of the proposed artists and participants (maximum of 2,000 characters including blanks in total). In the first application phase, the concept is more important than individual biographies.
The submission should therefore not exceed a total of 6,000 characters including spaces (approx. 900 words).
Furthermore we kindly ask you to visualize your project proposal or idea by selecting/designing one exemplary image (photo, sketch, collage, etc.) of approx. 20x30 cm size (DIN A4) that has to be uploaded as PDF-file with a maximum size of 50 MB.
Text overlengths are not taken into account! Multi-page PDF's are not considered!","February 11, 2026, 23:59 (CET, UTC+1)",2026-02-11,2026-09-24 09:58:10.908349+00:00
